    narr:narration """This narrative argues that [the hero video is a human moment, not a screen recording](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_15), resting on the rule [don't make people read text in a video](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_14). The core claim — [the human moment sells, not the text](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_18), echoed verbatim in a later advisor call as [the same antithesis framing](narr:Expr_6RealAdvisorCall20260728_25) — is operationalized as [a five-beat format running from real call footage to the expert's reaction](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_16), with a production path [assembled from existing call footage or a new short recording](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_17). The trust mechanism is [the `, aswritten` mode switch telling the AI to use real sources](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_20), so that [the viewer learns what this means by seeing the output change](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_21). The validation evidence is [a relayed account of the expert's reaction confirming the human moment sells better than any screen recording](narr:Expr_1RealFreyaPodcastSalesMotion20260323_8).""" ;
